"Resolver role — resolves an in-progress git merge conflict at a land boundary.

 Runs in a dedicated resolve worktree that already has a `git merge` IN PROGRESS
 (conflict markers present) — either main into a feature branch (full-pipeline
 feature->main land) or a parent feature branch into a story's impl branch (story
 impl->feature integrate). The resolver's one job is to resolve the conflicts and
 COMMIT the merge so the branch contains both sides; the workflow then re-syncs
 (clean) and lands via fast-forward.

 Scoped deliberately narrow: fix conflict markers only, do not add features."

Resolver subclass: Role

  classMethod: roleName [ ^'resolver' ]

  classMethod: roleDescription [
    ^'You resolve an in-progress git merge conflict so the branch can land.

Your worktree has a `git merge` already IN PROGRESS. Conflict markers (<<<<<<<,
=======, >>>>>>>) mark the clashes between this branch and the branch being
merged in.

MUST: Resolve every conflicted file so it is self-consistent and correct, keeping
      BOTH this branch''s work and the changes from the branch being merged in.
      Then commit the merge.
CAN: Read any file for context. Run the build/tests to confirm your resolution.
MUST NOT: Add new features. Abort the merge. Leave conflict markers. Skip committing.'
  ]

  classMethod: rolePpCommands [
    ^#(
      'pp observe <identity> <detail>           — report how you resolved the conflict'
      'pp read <category> [scope] [identity]    — read from the knowledge base'
      'pp dismiss                               — signal completion (AFTER committing)'
    )
  ]

  classMethod: hardCategories [ ^#('observation' 'decision' 'signal') ]
  classMethod: softCategories [ ^#('convention' 'fact') ]
  classMethod: contextBudget [ ^40 ]

  method: completionInstructions [
    ^'1. Inspect the conflict: git status ; git diff
2. For each conflicted file, resolve the markers so it keeps this branch''s work
   AND the changes from the branch being merged in, then: git add <file>
3. Conclude the merge: git commit --no-edit
   (Do NOT run git merge --abort — that would discard the land.)
4. Optionally verify: build and run the tests.
5. pp observe resolve "<what conflicted and how you resolved it>"
6. Dismiss: pp dismiss

WARNING: If you abort the merge or skip the commit, the branch cannot land and
the workflow will retry, then stop for a human.'
  ]
